</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L74" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Of course, Maple can produce these formulas for us.  To do this, we'll use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:eval" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">eval</Font></Hyperlink> command, which was introduced in Section 1.1.  Remember that the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:eval" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">eval</Font></Hyperlink> command accepts two arguments.  The first is an expression and the second is an equation specifying the substitution to be made.  For example,</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L75" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">eval(2*x+5,x=3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiM2</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L77" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">evaluates the expression <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> after substituting <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiM0YnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiM0YnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation> for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  In our case, the first argument will be the equation <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  Instead of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiZkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiZkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==</Equation>, we need to use our data <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">sumList</Font>.  So our first argument will be <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">sumList[n] = a*n^2+b*n+c</Font>.  With the second argument <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">n=2</Font>, say, Maple will replace the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">n</Font>'s with 2.  It will then look up the second entry in <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">sumList</Font> and simplify the right side of the equation for us.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L78" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">eval(sumList[n] = a*n^2 + b*n + c, n=2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LyIiJCwoSSJhRzYiIiIlSSJiR0YmIiIjSSJjR0YmIiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L80" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We can create a list of such equations with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:seq" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">seq</Font></Hyperlink> command.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L81" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">[seq(eval(sumList[n] = a*n^2 + b*n + c, n=N),N=1..10)];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">NywvIiIiLChJImFHNiJGJEkiYkdGJ0YkSSJjR0YnRiQvIiIkLChGJiIiJUYoIiIjRilGJC8iIicsKEYmIiIqRihGK0YpRiQvIiM1LChGJiIjO0YoRi1GKUYkLyIjOiwoRiYiI0RGKCIiJkYpRiQvIiNALChGJiIjT0YoRjBGKUYkLyIjRywoRiYiI1xGKCIiKEYpRiQvRj8sKEYmIiNrRigiIilGKUYkLyIjWCwoRiYiIyIpRihGMkYpRiQvIiNiLChGJiIkKyJGKEY0RilGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L83" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We now have a system of equations.  In particular, we have 10 equations in three variables.  You have probably seen systems of at least 2 and 3 equations in 2 or 3 variables in previous mathematics courses.  You can have Maple solve the system of equations by applying the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:solve" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">solve</Font></Hyperlink> command to the list.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L84" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">solve(<Label label="L81" view="label" executable="true"></Label>);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCUvSSJhRzYiIyIiIiIiIy9JImJHRiVGJi9JImNHRiUiIiE=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L86" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The first argument to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:solve" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">solve</Font></Hyperlink> can be a single equation or a set or list of equations.  If a set or list of equations is provided, as we did here, Maple will solve the equations as a system of simultaneous equations (<Font italic="true">i.e.</Font>, it finds values for the variables that make all the equations true simultaneously).  You can also provide an optional second argument: a name or a set or list of names.  If provided, Maple will solve the equations for those variables only, treating any other names as if they were constants.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L87" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">You may recall that to solve a system of equations in three unknowns, only three equations are required.  In this situation, having more equations is useful.  If we were wrong about the formula being quadratic but attempted to find coefficients with only three equations, Maple may still have found values for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiY0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiY0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> that satisfied the three equations we chose.  With ten equations, if the actual formula were not quadratic, there is a greater chance that no values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiY0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiY0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> would satisfy all ten equations.  In that case, Maple would have returned nothing to indicate the absence of a solution and indicating that our guess about the kind of formula was incorrect.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L90" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Let's review what we've done so far.  Our goal is to find a formula for the sum <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  We used Maple to compute a bunch of values of this sum and then graphed <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> versus the sum up to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  This graph suggested a quadratic formula, <Font italic="true">i.e.</Font>, one of the form <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> for some values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiY0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiY0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  We then used Maple's <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:solve" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">solve</Font></Hyperlink> command to determine that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  In other words, we've found the formula <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, which, of course, is the same as <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  Although we have found a formula, we have not yet <Font italic="true">proven</Font> anything, we've only made a conjecture.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L165" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">The induction proof</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L167" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">To prove that our formula is correct, we use mathematical induction.  First, let's make our formula into a functional operator.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L91" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L92" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">sumF := n -&gt; (1/2)*n^2 + (1/2)*n;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Zio2I0kibkc2IkYlNiRJKW9wZXJhdG9yR0YlSSZhcnJvd0dGJUYlLCYqJDkkIiIjIyIiIkYsRitGLUYlRiVGJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L94" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To complete the basis step of the induction, we need to see that the formula agrees with the sum for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L95" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">add(i,i=1..1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L96" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">sumF(1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L98" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">They are equal and the basis step is verified.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L99" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For the inductive step, we assume that the formula is correct for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and need to demonstrate that it is true for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  In Example 1, this was done by starting with the sum <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and applying the inductive hypothesis to the first <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> terms to obtain <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.   Then algebra is used to turn that expression into the formula evaluated at <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  With Maple, we can just check whether the expressions are the same.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L100" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The sum of the first <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> terms is <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L101" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">sumF(k) + (k+1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LCgqJEkia0c2IiIiIyMiIiJGJkYkIyIiJEYmRihGKA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L103" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">That used the inductive hypothesis.  We need to see if this is the same as <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L104" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">sumF(k+1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LCgqJCwmSSJrRzYiIiIiRidGJyIiIyNGJ0YoRiVGKUYpRic=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L106" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To check whether they are equal, we can form the equation obtained by equating the two expressions, apply <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:simplify#" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">simplify</Font></Hyperlink> to the equation to have Maple simplify both sides as much as it can, and then use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:evalb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">evalb</Font></Hyperlink> to find out if the equation is an identity.  Note that without <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:simplify" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">simplify</Font></Hyperlink>, Maple will not return the correct result.  All <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:evalb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">evalb</Font></Hyperlink> does in this case is to check to see if the two expressions are verbatim the same, it does not automatically do any algebra to check.  The application of <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:simplify" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">simplify</Font></Hyperlink> causes Maple to do the symbolic algebra that allows <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:evalb" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">evalb</Font></Hyperlink> to recognize the truth of the equation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L107" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">evalb(simplify(sumF(k) + (k+1) = sumF(k+1)));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L73" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This indicates that the inductive step is verified, and hence the formula is correct.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L109" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Summation Example 2</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L110" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">As a second example of using Maple to find and prove summation formulae, consider the sum <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  For this example, we won't go through the process of computing values and graphing as we did above.  That is a very valuable process, and we strongly recommend that you go through it yourself with a few examples.  But Maple includes a command that will give us the result.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L173" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Using sum to determine the formula</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L111" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We used the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">add</Font></Hyperlink> command for calculating the numeric sum of a sequence of numbers.  Maple also has a <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:sum" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">sum</Font></Hyperlink> command, which is used for symbolic summation.  To calculate <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, we use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">add</Font></Hyperlink> as we did in the previous example.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L112" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">add(i^2*(i+1)!,i=1..10);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IistVyw2Vg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L114" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">(Note the use of the exclamation mark for the computation of factorial.  You can also use the command <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:factorial" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">factorial</Font></Hyperlink>, if you prefer.)</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L115" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">add</Font></Hyperlink> command is designed specifically for numeric computations like the above.  For symbolic calculations, as in <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:sum" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">sum</Font></Hyperlink> command is used.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L116" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">sum(i^2*(i+1)!,i=1..n);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LCYqJiwmSSJuRzYiIiIiISIiRidGJy1JKmZhY3RvcmlhbEclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc2IywmRiVGJyIiI0YnRidGJ0YuRic=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L118" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The syntax of the two commands is nearly identical.  The first argument is the expression to be summed in terms of an index variable.  The second argument is an equation that sets the index variable equal to a range.  The difference is that the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:sum" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">sum</Font></Hyperlink> command allows the range to be symbolic, for example  <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">1..n</Font> or <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">0..infinity</Font>.  Note that the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:sum" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">sum</Font></Hyperlink> command could be used for the numeric calculation as well, but <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">add</Font></Hyperlink> is optimized for that purpose.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L119" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Similarly, Maple includes <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">mul</Font></Hyperlink> for computing numeric products and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:product" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">product</Font></Hyperlink> for computing symbolic products.  These commands have the same syntax as <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">add</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:sum" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">sum</Font></Hyperlink>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L174" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">The induction proof</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L844" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Now that the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:sum" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">sum</Font></Hyperlink> command has determined the formula </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L846" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L120" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">we will use Maple to help us prove it.  Even though we can be very confident that Maple has given us a correct formula, applying a Maple command is not the same as a proof.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L121" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">As before, we'll define a functional operator based on the formula.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L122" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">sumF2 := n -&gt; (n-1)*(n+2)! + 2;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Zio2I0kibkc2IkYlNiRJKW9wZXJhdG9yR0YlSSZhcnJvd0dGJUYlLCYqJiwmOSQiIiIhIiJGLUYtLUkqZmFjdG9yaWFsRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRzYjLCZGLEYtIiIjRi1GLUYtRjRGLUYlRiVGJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L124" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For the basis step of the induction, we need to see that the formula holds for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  The value of the sum for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L125" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">1^2*(1+1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L127" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">And the formula applied to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L128" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">sumF2(1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L130" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The basis step is verified.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L858" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For the inductive step, we assume that the formula is correct for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  That is, we assume that</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L853" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L850" drawlabel="true" redirect-target="L851">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="sum(factorial(i(i+1)^2), i = 1 .. k) = factorial((k-1)(k+2))+2" display="">Ly1JJHN1bUc2JCUqcHJvdGVjdGVkR0koX3N5c2xpYkc2IjYkLUkqZmFjdG9yaWFsR0YmNiMqJC1JImlHRig2IywmRi8iIiJGMkYyIiIjL0YvO0YySSJrR0YoLCYtRis2Iy0sJkY2RjIhIiJGMjYjLCZGNkYyRjNGMkYyRjNGMg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Ly1JJHN1bUc2JCUqcHJvdGVjdGVkR0koX3N5c2xpYkc2IjYkLUkqZmFjdG9yaWFsR0YmNiMqJC1JImlHRig2IywmRi8iIiJGMkYyIiIjL0YvO0YySSJrR0YoLCYtRis2IywmLUY2NiMsJkY2RjJGM0YyRjIhIiJGMkYyRjNGMg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L856" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L860" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We need to show that the formula works for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  Now, </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L862" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L131" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Using the inductive hypothesis that the formula is correct for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, this is</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L132" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">sumF2(k) + (k+1)^2*((k+1)+1)!;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LCgqJiwmSSJrRzYiIiIiISIiRidGJy1JKmZhY3RvcmlhbEclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc2IywmRiVGJyIiI0YnRidGJ0YuRicqJiwmRiVGJ0YnRidGLkYpRidGJw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L134" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We need to check to see if this is the same as the formula applied to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L135" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">sumF2(k+1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LCYqJkkia0c2IiIiIi1JKmZhY3RvcmlhbEclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc2IywmRiRGJiIiJEYmRiZGJiIiI0Ym</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L142" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">evalb(simplify(sumF2(k) + (k+1)^2*((k+1)+1)!=sumF2(k+1)));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L144" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This completes the induction and proves the correctness of the formula.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L145" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Divisibility</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L146" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">For a final example, we'll see how Maple can be used to help prove results about divisibility.  In Example 8 from Section 5.1 of the text, it was shown that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is divisible by 3 for all positive integers <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  Exercise 33 asks you to prove that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is divisible by 5 for all positive integers.  These two facts suggest that perhaps <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is divisible by 7 for all positive integers <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  Let's see how Maple can help us prove this fact.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L147" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We begin by creating a functional operator to represent the expression <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L148" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">divExpr := n -&gt; n^7 - n;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Zio2I0kibkc2IkYlNiRJKW9wZXJhdG9yR0YlSSZhcnJvd0dGJUYlLCYqJDkkIiIoIiIiRishIiJGJUYlRiU=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L150" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The basis case is <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  For <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, the expression is </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L151" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">divExpr(0);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIh</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
